  - benchmark
  - MCP
abstract: >-
  GeoSpark wires ground-truth spatial tools into LLMs via the Model Context
  Protocol, and ships GeoSpark Bench (535 questions across topology, distance,
  change detection, multi-step reasoning, and multimodal sensor interpretation)
  to measure how much that wiring matters. The v2 study evaluates nine models
  in three buckets and finds that bare-LLM accuracy on numeric distance
  questions varies dramatically by model and vendor (1-95 percent) rather than
  being uniformly broken. The smallest open-weight tier 3.8B-9B mostly fails
  outright; one open-weight 20B model performs comparably to frontier; the
  three frontier APIs themselves disagree by 70 percentage points on identical
  questions. With GeoSpark tools attached, every model that can orchestrate a
  tool call lands in a 76-96 percent band, which collapses the vendor lottery
  into a deterministic engine call. Two further findings of independent
  interest: per-predicate decomposition reveals model bias that aggregate
  topology accuracy hides, and Chain-of-Thought is not a free intervention
  (helps some models, hurts others, by significant margins).
